+### Range Is Not the Same as Precision
+
+
+[markdown · ba18_d84b93d8c73d]
+An integer type represents whole numbers in a fixed range. A floating-point type can cover a much larger range but has only a limited number of significant binary digits. Thus a value can fit the *range* of `float` without its exact integer value being representable. The compiler permits the conversion; that permission does not promise exactness.
+
+General patterns (schematic):
+
+```text
+DestinationType converted = source;       // only if an implicit conversion exists
+DestinationType converted = (DestinationType)source;
+int parsed = int.Parse(text);              // interpret text, rather than cast it
+```
+
+The type name in parentheses requests a conversion of the expression that follows it. `Parse` instead calls a library method and returns a new value. Neither operation validates the business meaning of that value.[^conversion-precision]
+
+
+[code · ba18_215ab326e744]
+using System;
+int exact = 16777217;
+float rounded = exact;
+Console.WriteLine($"integer={exact}");
+Console.WriteLine($"float={rounded:R}");
+Console.WriteLine($"same value={(double)exact == (double)rounded}");
+
+
+[markdown · ba18_817b28b86a77]
+The output is `integer=16777217`, `float=16777216`, and `same value=False`. Both values are well inside the floating-point range, but this `float` cannot distinguish neighboring integers at that magnitude. The `R` format displays a round-trippable representation. Converting both sides to `double` for comparison makes the lost unit visible instead of converting the integer to the same rounded `float` again.
+
+| Operation | Question to ask |
+| --- | --- |
+| Implicit numeric conversion | Is this permitted, and is the original value exactly representable? |
+| Explicit numeric cast | What rounding/truncation and range rules apply? |
+| Parsing text | Does the text obey the numeric format? |
+| Domain validation | Is the resulting number allowed for this task? |
+
+Predict the result with `exact = 123`: that integer is exactly representable in `float`, so the comparison becomes `True`. A successful small example does not prove every conversion of that type is exact.
 
 
