+The solution prints `20.00`, `left=12.50, right=7.50`, and `7.50`. Returning `left` passes no sum test except a zero right operand, so a single convenient test could conceal the defect. Constructing a new value keeps the inputs unchanged and reuses the constructor's nonnegative-value contract. Arithmetic overflow is still possible for sufficiently large decimal values; this example does not claim unlimited range. A real multi-currency type would need to check currency compatibility before adding.
